Wrote /workspace/kv/server.py (stdlib only: http.server ThreadingHTTPServer, json, threading). Data is kept in memory behind a threading.Lock and persisted on every write via temp-file + os.replace (atomic, so a crash can't corrupt DATAFILE); the lock plus ThreadingHTTPServer means concurrent requests never lose writes.

How I tested it (curl against a live server on port 8399, then stopped it):
- GET /kv on empty store → 200 {"keys": []}
- PUT /kv/foo {"a":1} → 201; PUT again {"a":2} → 200; GET → {"a":2}
- GET missing key → 404; DELETE existing → 204; DELETE again → 404
